index 8e26a1a3d19b3f60c5ca8dd5acb13ed98c2895c9..40371d1285720ea54e1eb6fcd28993235d564780 100644
--- a/CONTRACT.md
+++ b/CONTRACT.md
@@ -338,6 +338,34 @@ only because the definition cannot be named directly. Those lower to an ordinary
 `core`'s `panic_impl` (the `#[panic_handler]`) is the case that matters; the allocator shims have
 the same shape.
 
+### `#[track_caller]`
+
+A function rustc says `requires_caller_location` takes the caller's `Location` as one trailing
+parameter, `$loc`; a call to it appends the call's location, or, inside another tracked function,
+that function's own `$loc`, so a chain reports its outermost untracked caller as native code does.
+A `fn` pointer call cannot pass the location, so a pointer to a tracked function names the
+`ReifyShim` rustc builds (`Instance::resolve_for_fn_ptr`), which reports the function's own
+definition. A shim shares its definition and type with the function it wraps, so the producer keys
+a shim's `ItemId` by the whole instance: the link step's per-item facts (the item index a tracked
+body consults to decide whether a callee takes its `$loc`, the flat ABI plan) never conflate the
+two.
+
+Limits, all present before the shim and identity rules above:
+
+- A tracked function that makes a *virtual* call to a tracked trait method forwards its `$loc`, and
+  JIR-S records that call's location as `None`, which also means "takes none". The JIR lowering
+  resolves the two from the callee's item-index entry, which a virtual callee does not have, so it
+  declines the body: the baseline text is right, but a release profile refuses the fallback when the
+  body owes cleanup, and the program does not compile. The fix is for the producer to say "forward"
+  explicitly in the call (a JIR-S/JIR-R schema change).
+- rustc's MIR inliner (`JSC_RUST_MIR_INLINE=on`) can place a tracked call inside a body it inlined;
+  rustc then walks the inlined scopes (`mir::Body::caller_location_span`), the producer spells the
+  call's own span on both lanes. Release profiles lower user code from MIR captured before
+  inlining and are right; cleanup-off modes report a location inside the inlined callee.
+- A tracked function item handed to a host marker as a callable value is called without a location
+  unless the program also takes a `fn` pointer to it (v1 never passes one); a keyed-list callable
+  that is a tracked function item is declined (`keyed.*-body-mismatch`).
+
 ### `#[panic_handler]` crates must not format
 
 A `#![no_std]` crate's panic handler may read `PanicInfo::location()` — the `Location` arrives
